<p>Dallas is not on the coast, and most people do not think of it as flood country. The NOAA record says otherwise. When slow-moving storms stall over the Metroplex, several inches of rain can fall in an hour or two. Storm drains back up, creeks like White Rock, Turtle, Five Mile, and Duck Creek leave their banks, and underpasses fill with water. Flash flood damage can reach homes and businesses that have never flooded before.</p>

<p>The harder part often comes after the water recedes, when owners learn that their homeowners policy treats flood very differently from other water damage. This guide explains the difference, how flood insurance works, and what to do if your Dallas County property takes on water.</p>

<h2>Flash flooding in Dallas County by the numbers</h2>

<p>The NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) Storm Events Database lists 305 flash flood reports for Dallas County from 1996 through June 2026, plus 22 flood reports. Flash flood reports cluster in late spring and early summer, but they appear in every month.</p>

