<p><em>Source: NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database, Dallas County, Texas. Counts are reports, not storm days; one flood event can produce many reports.</em></p>

<p>The recent trend is worth noting. 2025 had 44 flash flood reports, the most of any year in the record, and 2026 already had 21 by the end of June. As with any NOAA count, more reporting through social media and emergency managers accounts for part of that increase, but the pattern lines up with what Dallas residents have seen on their own streets.</p>

<h2>Major Dallas County flash floods</h2>

<h3>August 21–22, 2022</h3>

<p>The August 2022 flood is the benchmark for modern Dallas flooding. NOAA reports that upwards of 15 inches of rain fell in some areas, and that numerous roads, vehicles, homes, and buildings were flooded and heavily damaged. In Balch Springs, which NOAA describes as the hardest-hit area with over 15 inches measured, residents were rescued from homes along Hickory Tree Road in knee- to thigh-deep water. In <a href="/service-areas/mesquite/">Mesquite</a>, more than 10 inches fell in a few hours and I-635 at Scyene Road was closed by high water. In east Dallas, 2 feet of water entered an apartment complex and knocked a front door off its hinges. Dallas police reported at least 42 roads with high water calls.</p>

<h3>May 29, 2015</h3>

<p>After a very wet May, 3 to 8 inches of rain fell across the county. NOAA reports that Duck Creek in Garland overflowed its banks and affected properties along the creek, with cars swept from driveways near Glenbrook and Carroll Drives. Loop 12 between Irving Boulevard and I-30 was closed for nearly four days, and the first floor of an apartment complex near South Belt Line Road flooded, forcing residents to evacuate.</p>

<h3>June 11, 2009</h3>

<p>Training thunderstorms parked over Dallas County that morning. NOAA reports that Turtle Creek overflowed its banks in <a href="/service-areas/highland-park/">Highland Park</a>, where about six homes were flooded and a chest-deep water rescue took place where Fitzhugh and Wycliff cross the creek. Dallas Fire and Rescue had conducted 48 water rescues by 7:30 a.m., homes were evacuated near Sylvan Avenue and McBroom Street, and lightning strikes knocked out a pump at a Dallas pumping station serving parts of west Dallas, making the flooding worse.</p>

<h3>Recent events</h3>

<ul>
