<h2>How flood insurance works</h2>

<h3>NFIP and private flood policies</h3>

<p>Most flood insurance in Texas is written through the National Flood Insurance Program, which FEMA manages and insurance agents sell. Private flood policies are also available. According to FloodSmart.gov, NFIP residential policies offer up to $250,000 in building coverage and up to $100,000 in contents coverage, purchased separately. Private policies may offer different limits and terms.</p>

<h3>The 30-day waiting period</h3>

<p>NFIP policies typically take effect 30 days after purchase, with limited exceptions. That means you cannot wait for a forecast of heavy rain over Dallas to buy coverage. If your property is near White Rock Creek, Turtle Creek, Five Mile or Ten Mile creeks, Rowlett Creek, or in a low spot that collects runoff, it is worth reviewing your flood coverage well before May.</p>

<h3>What NFIP typically covers and limits</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Building coverage</strong> generally includes the structure and foundation, electrical and plumbing systems, HVAC equipment, water heaters, built-in appliances, and permanently installed flooring and cabinets.</li>
<li><strong>Contents coverage</strong> is separate and covers personal belongings, often at actual cash value under the standard dwelling form.</li>
<li><strong>Limits apply</strong> to items below the lowest elevated floor and in basements. Most Dallas homes are slab-on-grade, but older pier-and-beam homes in areas like Lakewood, the M Streets, and Oak Cliff have crawlspaces, where coverage can be more limited.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses</strong> are generally not covered by NFIP policies, unlike many homeowners policies.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Rebuilding after a flood</h3>

<p>If a building in a mapped floodplain is substantially damaged, the local floodplain administrator may require it to be elevated or otherwise brought up to current floodplain rules before it is rebuilt. NFIP policies include Increased Cost of Compliance coverage that may help with some of those costs when the community determines the building is substantially damaged. Inside the city, permits for flood repairs go through the City of Dallas Development Services Department; suburbs such as Mesquite, Garland, and Balch Springs have their own offices. Ask about permit and floodplain requirements before rebuilding.</p>

<p>These are general descriptions. Your Standard Flood Insurance Policy or private flood policy controls, and it is worth reading before you need it.</p>

<h2>Filing a flood claim: practical steps</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not walk or drive through floodwater. Turn off power at the breaker only if you can do it safely, and call Oncor or Atmos Energy for electrical or gas hazards.</li>
<li><strong>Document the water line</strong> on walls, cabinets, and appliances with photos and video before cleanup. Measure the depth if you can.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph damaged items</strong> before you throw them away, and keep samples of flooring, carpet, and drywall when practical. Ask your adjuster before discarding large items.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> to your flood insurer and, if any part of the damage could be wind, hail, or plumbing related, to your homeowners insurer as well.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying out</strong> quickly to limit mold, and keep receipts for fans, dehumidifiers, and mitigation services.</li>
<li><strong>Watch the proof of loss deadline.</strong> NFIP claims generally require a signed proof of loss within a set period after the loss, and FEMA sometimes extends it after major events. Ask your flood adjuster for the current deadline in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Keep organized records</strong> using our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a>.</li>
</ol>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><span class="callout-title">Before you tear out drywall</span><p>Cleanup is necessary, but take photos of every room and keep a written list of what was removed and why. Once materials are gone, it becomes difficult to show the extent of the damage or to argue that more of the structure was affected.</p></div>

<h2>When a flood claim is denied or underpaid</h2>

<p>Flood claims are often disputed over the cause of the water, how far the damage extends, and whether items below the lowest floor are covered. A homeowners claim for storm-related water can be denied when the insurer decides the water was flood. If you disagree, ask for the reasoning in writing and review the policy language with someone who knows it. Our page on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> explains common next steps, and questions about appeals or lawsuits should go to a Texas attorney.</p>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help</h2>

<p>Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps Dallas County property owners with <a href="/services/flood-damage-claims/">flood damage claims</a> and the water damage claims that often come with them. That can include documenting the loss, separating flood damage from covered wind, hail, or plumbing damage, preparing a detailed estimate, and presenting the claim. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Coverage always depends on the policy and the facts, and no result is guaranteed. If you are unsure which policy should respond to your water damage, a Dallas Public Adjuster review can help you sort it out.</p>
